  "findings": "No rigorous counterexample or proof was obtained. A finite identity can be checked directly: the total number of weak compositions is binom(K+R-1,R-1). Fixing j pair totals with prefix sum h, a positive word of length L with total w has multiplicity [x^w](sum_{b=1}^{B_0}(b+1)x^b)^L, while the prefix and suffix contribute binom(h+2j-1,2j-1) and binom(K-h-w+R-2j-2L-1,R-2j-2L-1), respectively. Dividing their sum by the total count gives the corresponding exact local probability, including endpoint conditioning. This identity does not control the joint lower tail of marked blocks. Likewise, for fixed u, the event that the first u raw coordinates vanish has probability binom(K+R-u-1,R-u-1)/binom(K+R-1,R-1), tending to (1+beta)^(-u) along K/R -> beta. That supplies no refutation: it concerns only a fixed prefix, whereas suppression of every marked block is a global bridge event. No admissible actual-array event with probability asymptotically larger than order 1/R and with zero or sublogarithmically many C_q blocks was established. The supplied local reductions therefore neither prove nor refute the claimed estimate.",
  "claim_status": "OPEN",
  "first_missing_step": "A strictly narrower sufficient estimate is the following marked lower-tail bound. Writing N_R=sum_{q in Q_R} 1_{C_q}, prove that there exist A>1/lambda, C_0, and R_0, uniform over every sufficiently large actual accessible array and its declared conditioning data, such that P(N_R < A log R | S_R=K and exact first-passage data) <= C_0/R for R>=R_0. Indeed, on N_R >= A log R, exp(-lambda N_R) <= R^{-lambda A}; choosing A=2/lambda would yield the target from this bound. The exact coefficient formula above does not imply this inequality because it is single-block/local, while the required estimate must control correlations among disjoint blocks, affine boundary shifts, even-time sampling, low entries, and amplified resonances simultaneously.",
  "unexpected_findings": [
    "Escalation candidate: the phrase \"exact first-passage data\" does not specify whether the law remains the uniform weak-composition law conditioned only on S_R=K or is further conditioned on additional data. If it is further conditioning, an explicit conditional law or comparison bound is required before the stated uniform-composition identities can be applied."
